<p>On 30 September 1895 a twelve-year-old boy from a mountain village in Mount Lebanon walked into the Josiah Quincy School in Boston. He had been in the United States for about three months. He spoke no English. He had never sat in a school of this kind in his life. The clerk wrote his name into the register as <em>Kahlil Gibran</em>, and the boy - whose name was Jubran Khalil Jubran - kept that spelling on every book he published for the next thirty-six years.</p>

<p>That is the first thing to notice. His career in English began with an official misspelling of his own name, made by an institution that had already decided it could not pronounce him. Everything that followed was built on top of that small, ordinary humiliation and the decision not to argue with it.</p>

