<h2>Bsharri, Before Anything</h2>

<p>He was born into a Maronite Christian family in the village of Bsharri, high in the Kadisha Valley, in the last decades of Ottoman rule. His father Khalil worked first in an apothecary and then for a local Ottoman-appointed administrator, and in 1891, while acting as a tax collector, he was removed from his post, investigated and imprisoned for embezzlement. The family's property was confiscated. Gibran was eight.</p>

<p>What schooling he had in Lebanon was village schooling: reading and writing Arabic, and the Bible. That gave him the spoken language of his region and the liturgical register of his church. It did not give him literary Arabic - the classical written language of the newspapers and the poets. He would have to go back for that, and later he did.</p>

<h2>Boston, South End, 1895</h2>

<p>The family emigrated in 1895 and settled in the South End of Boston, at the time the second-largest Syrian-Lebanese community in the United States. His mother worked as a seamstress. The children took whatever work there was, including peddling. Within weeks of arrival, Gibran was in school.</p>

<p>School officials placed him in a special class for immigrants to learn English, which is the same institutional solution every American city has used before and since, and which has one great virtue and one great flaw. The virtue is immersion with no translation: the only language in the room is the one you do not have. The flaw is that it treats a twelve-year-old with a mother tongue, a religion and a village as a blank.</p>

<p>What saved him was drawing. A teacher noticed his work and presented him to F. Holland Day, a photographer and publisher with real standing in the Boston art world. Day took an interest, showed the drawings, and introduced the boy to people who could help him. By fifteen he had a patron in the city's artistic establishment, and the English that mattered to him at that point was the English of pictures.</p>

<h2>The Return: Three Years of Arabic</h2>

<p>Then, in 1898, his family sent him back.</p>

<p>The reason is usually given plainly - to be properly educated - and the effect was a three-year course in Arabic literature at the Coll&egrave;ge de la Sagesse, a Maronite-run institute in Beirut, where he also learned French. He arrived at fifteen with village Arabic that had been sitting unused in an English-speaking city for three years, and left at eighteen with the classical register and a second European language.</p>

<p>This is the part of his education that gets skipped, and it is the part a language learner should read twice. He did not choose between his languages. He went home to repair the one that was atrophying while the other was still forming, and he came back with both. The pattern is familiar to anyone from a heritage-speaking family: the language of the kitchen is fluent and the language of the newspaper is foreign, and the gap does not close by itself. He closed it in a classroom, deliberately, at fifteen, while his English was still imperfect.</p>

<blockquote>He was not choosing a language. He was collecting two, in the only order available to him.</blockquote>

<h2>1902: The Year the Family Died</h2>

<p>His sister Sultana died of tuberculosis in 1902, at fourteen. Gibran sailed home. Within the following year he lost his half-brother Boutros and then his mother. He was twenty years old, in Boston, with one surviving sister, Mariana, who supported them both for a time with her work at a dressmaker's shop.</p>

<p>Whatever plan the family had for his Arabic education ended there. He stayed, and English became the language of his adult life - not by conviction, but because that is where he was when everybody died. Most language biographies are decided like this: not by aptitude or method, but by which country you happen to be in when the news arrives.</p>

<h2>Mary Haskell, and the Admission</h2>

<p>In January 1904 his drawings were exhibited for the first time, at F. Holland Day's studio in Boston. At that exhibition he met Mary Haskell, the headmistress of a girls' school in the city, nine years his senior. She was impressed by the work, and within a few years she was funding him: seventy-five dollars a month to study art in Paris from 1908 to 1910, which was a serious sum at the time - the equivalent of roughly two thousand dollars a month in modern terms.</p>

<p>She also became the person who taught him English prose.</p>

<p>The detail that matters comes from around 1904, in a letter to Haskell: Gibran admitted that he still thought primarily in Arabic when he tried to write in English. Nine years in the country, three of them in an English-language school, and the inner voice had not changed language. That is the private-speech problem in its purest form - comprehension and production had arrived, but the medium of thought had not.</p>

<p>Haskell worked on it with him for the next two decades: as scribe at first, helping with words and phrases, then as editor of everything he wrote in English. Her own diary entry from August 1914 records her ambition for him: <em>"It will not be long before he will be so a master of English that he need look nothing over with anybody."</em></p>

<p>In January 1917 he wrote to her:</p>

<blockquote>You see, Mary, I go to your school, too, and I am sure that I could not have written a word in English if it were not for you.</blockquote>

<p>And in April 1918, while finishing <em>The Madman</em>: <em>"how can anything of mine be really English without your help?"</em></p>

<p>He kept sending her manuscripts even after she moved to Savannah in 1923. This is not a footnote to his biography; it is the mechanism of it. He wrote in a second language for twenty years and he never pretended the second language was self-sufficient. He had an editor who was a native speaker, he used her, and he said so in writing.</p>

<h2>From Arabic Fame to English Fame</h2>

<p>His first published work, in 1905, was in Arabic: <em>A Profile of the Art of Music</em>, printed in New York. <em>Nymphs of the Valley</em> followed in 1906, and <em>Spirits Rebellious</em> in 1908 - a book critical enough of religious and secular authority that it was publicly burned in the Beirut marketplace. By the time he settled in New York in 1911 he was a known Arabic writer with a dangerous reputation.</p>

<p>In 1920 he re-founded the Pen League with fellow Mahjar poets - Amin Rihani, Mikhail Naimy, Elia Abu Madi among them - a movement to modernise Arabic poetry from exile. So the year he helped reorganise Arabic literature, he was also writing, in English, the book that would make him famous outside it.</p>

<p>He had begun <em>The Prophet</em> in 1912. In May 1918 he showed Haskell the first draft, then titled <em>The Counsels</em>. It was published in September 1923: 107 pages, twenty-six prose poems, twelve drawings by the author, brought out by Alfred A. Knopf with little promotion.</p>

<p>It has not been out of print since. By 2012 it had sold more than nine million copies in the American edition alone, and it has been translated into more than a hundred languages. In 1918, when it was still a manuscript, he had written to Haskell about the draft:</p>

<blockquote>Do you notice how full these things are of what we have said in talking together - sometimes years ago... There's nothing in them that hasn't come from our talks. Talking about them with you has made them clear to me.</blockquote>

<h2>What the English Actually Looks Like</h2>

<p>Read a page of <em>The Prophet</em> next to a page of almost any English poetry of 1923 and the difference is obvious. The sentences are short. The vocabulary is common. The syntax repeats. There is almost no subordinate clause that runs longer than a breath.</p>

<p>Critics have read this as simplicity, or as a defect, and there is a long tradition of English-literature opinion treating Gibran as a writer whose prose does not meet the standards of the language. But read it as the work of a man who learned English at twelve, wrote in it from his thirties, and had a native-speaker editor, and the style looks like something else: a deliberate constraint. He chose the register he could control completely, and inside it he could say what he wanted.</p>

<blockquote>No man can reveal to you aught but that which already lies half asleep in the dawning of your knowledge. The teacher who walks in the shadow of the temple, among his followers, gives not of his wisdom but rather of his faith and his lovingness.</blockquote>

<p>That is from the chapter on teaching, and it is also a fair description of how he learned: nothing was transmitted to him that he did not already half-have, and what he needed was somebody - a teacher, an editor, a village - to make him finish the sentence.</p>

<h2>Five Things a Learner Can Copy</h2>

<p><strong>1. Accept the immersion class, even the badly run one.</strong> The special class at Josiah Quincy gave him no translation, no grammar explanation, and no dignity. It also gave him a year of total exposure at an age when that still works. Immersion is not a method you have to approve of to benefit from.</p>

<p><strong>2. Go back for the language you only speak.</strong> He had fluent village Arabic and no literary Arabic, and at fifteen he spent three years fixing exactly that gap. Heritage speakers who can talk to their grandmother but cannot read a newspaper are in the same position, and the fix is the same: formal instruction in the register you are missing, taken seriously, in a classroom, for years.</p>

<p><strong>3. Notice which language you think in - and treat it as data.</strong> Nine years into English, by his own account, his inner voice was still Arabic. That is not a moral failure. It is a measurement, and it told him precisely which operation the new language had not yet taken over. He then spent twenty years writing in English until it had.</p>

<p><strong>4. Get an editor and admit it.</strong> He wrote, in letters, that he could not have written a word in English without Mary Haskell. Nobody reads those letters as a confession of weakness. Find the person who will mark your writing, and keep sending it after they move away.</p>

<p><strong>5. Write inside the register you control.</strong> Short sentences and common words are not a reduced version of a language. They are a style, and they carried him further than most syntactic ambition carries anybody. If your English is 1,500 words deep, write 1,500-word-deep English, and write it well.</p>

<h2>Bsharri, Again</h2>

<p>He died in New York on 10 April 1931, aged forty-eight. His body was taken back to Bsharri, and he had left the royalties on his books to the village he was born in. The Gibran Museum stands there now, in the old monastery where he is buried.</p>

<p>He spent his life moving between two languages and never resolved the tension. The Arabic readers thought the English books were thinner. The English readers did not know the Arabic ones existed. What survived - a hundred languages, nine million copies, a book that has not been out of print for a century - was written by a man who arrived in a Boston classroom at twelve with no English, was registered under a misspelling of his name, and decided that the misspelling would do.</p>

